  {
    "episode_id": 82263,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82263.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. Well, thanks for that. You're right. I mean, look, I I studied, I did my PhD on international relations, but what I focused on was about conflict management resolution. It wasn't specific to the Middle East, it just happens that a lot of the conflicts were happening there. I think for me, it helps me understand a framework of how to understand a problem. I was trained as a strategic planner, trying to understand the magnitude of the problem that we face. and try to conceptualize the magnitude of the solution needed to address that problem. I think that that allows me to look at things oftentimes a little bit differently than the conversation on Capitol Hill is done. I work in what's arguably the most reactionary building in America. It is constantly reacting to the headlines, to the latest crisis. The issues that rise up to hearings are things that are front page news. But what we often know is that a lot of the important things don't always make it to the front page news. So I try to be somebody that thinks strategically, tries to lay out what are our objectives, what does success look like. And then the work that we did with counter-ISIS and other efforts The other things that really came to bear wasn't just about the development of strategy, but recognizing that where does American global power come from? What are our strategic advantages? So much of that being about coalition building. And that's something that is fundamental to how I see what 21st century power looks like. The last thing I'll just say, in terms of how my work is shaped by my experience, is I very much came to understand the power of non-state actors and transnational actors, not just about dealing with Westphalian states. So much of foreign policy and so much of how it's conceptualized on Capitol Hill is about government to government. But when I dealt with so many non-sayed actors, whether they are terrorist groups like ISIS or civil society, I do think that so much of the answer, especially when you're dealing with a region like the Indo-Pacific, has to include greater people-to-people, greater engagements at a level beyond just the high-level leaders of the nations. And that's something that I think we need to do better at as a country in terms of mobilizing.",
