  {
    "episode_id": 82263,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82263.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, look, I mean, it starts with just the deep challenges we face when it comes to a budget, you know, why, you know, why I was here in DC this week, you know, our failure to be able to put forward a budget even for the next year, let alone trying to think through doing that more forward for a number of years, you know, work having worked at the Pentagon. state department, a number of different federal departments and agencies. It's so difficult for them to conceptualize and follow through on a strategy when just by virtue of how we operate is done so piecemeal. And so that's one aspect of it. But just beyond that, I find that You know, okay, so like I said, it's, you know, the most generalist job, sorry, it's the most reactionary building, but what I also say is I also work in what's arguably the most generalist job in America. And that's a challenge. You know, I went from being a subject matter expert on, you know, counter-ISIS in the Middle East to now having to deal with so many other issues. So just the bandwidth of attention is difficult. So as a result, it does affect our capacity to be able to lay out a vision. and be able to actually try to implement and execute that. And I think that's something that we have to get better at if we want Article 1 branch of government to operate in the way that it should be.",
