    "turn_text": "Well, and I think it's always important to put it in the context of just a few years earlier, which is we saw probably some of the lowest points in the Korea-Japan relationship that I've seen there in my lifetime. So to see where it's at now and the speed with which I change happened is quite remarkable. And I agree with you in general that yes, I do think that that has created this great openness level cooperation and does feel like a real commitment to that at the government level. I think, you know, drawing in what I was saying about the people to people, that still needs to be built out. There's no question that there are still deep-set concerns and challenges. I hear it from people that I talk to in Korea and in Japan as well. I know that's not going to be moved. There's some deep-seated issues that aren't going to necessarily be able to all be addressed in ways that are perfect for everybody. But I do think that starting on this capacity of finding areas of commonality and building out is productive and is something that hopefully will help recognize that their shared interests going forward and their shared prosperity going forward comes from working together. But the other thing I guess I'll just say, is it is really important, though, and that we try to tell the story of what that trilateral, what that relationship looks like. I mean, even just on a bilateral level, you know, we had just the other year, you know, the 70th anniversary of the Armistice of the Korean War, we had a state visit from the South Korean president. And we talked a lot about the last 70 years. But there really needs to be greater effort to explain what the next 70 years is going to look like. Why is it that we're putting this effort to pull together Korea, Japan, and the United States? And I think that that story needs to be told, not just for the purposes of the population in Korea and Japan, but here in the United States. I talked to a lot of people in the United States, and they're like, well, What is the valley here and you see that manifest by questions of you know that we saw during the Trump administration of you know the the amount of money that the U.S. and South Korea will pay into their military partnership, questions about U.S. troops on the peninsula, things like that. So I think it's important that we tell that story about what these relationships",
