    "turn_text": "Yeah, and look, we're seeing that in large swath across East Asia, China also facing major demographic changes in that way. And I think that that's why it's important to be looking towards that future, trying to understand, well, what does that mean in terms of just where the economic growth is going to happen when you see a lot more manufacturing capabilities in Vietnam and Indonesia and elsewhere? And you start to get a sense of, like, what is the Indo-Pacific going to look like 20, 30 years from now with these population changes happening in that kind of vein? And what kind of strain is going to put on leaders of these nations? And we saw when we came to the last presidential race in South Korea, how so much of that was drawn and built off of grievances from younger people, younger generations in You see that in Japan, you see that in China and elsewhere. I do think that that youth bulge and that challenge that we face when it comes to the next generation, we see here in the United States as well. And that is something that is driving a lot of the discontent that is fueling some of the different policy decisions that are being made. And so yes, I do think that that's really critical. And something that the United States needs to pay attention to, to understand, you always pass the puck to where the hockey player is",
