    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, as I said earlier, I'm a big fan of coalition building and I think that that's key. I think it speaks to just the topography of the Indo-Pacific, that it's going to be different than how coalition building in the transatlantic is going to be conceptualized, and we shouldn't necessarily compare the two. Lessons can be learned, but there's just much more complexity in terms of identities, in terms of relationships, and just frankly, just a sheer space too. I mean, it's pretty extraordinary. But I think it gets to what What a lot of this discussion is culminating at is just what comes next and undoubtedly this question about what the relationship is between the United States and China and these other countries in China and kind of framing that broader direction. I think what you see from this agreement for these different types of structures is a sense that from a lot of the nations that they want to maintain a multi-polarity in the Indo-Pacific. that they don't want to see a hegemonic power of any sort. be able to emerge in a regional and frankly global sense that the Indo-Pacific is one that is critically important, that it maintains that type of topography. And I think that that's a commitment, what those dynamics are and what are the exact contours of that yet to be determined. But I think that there is a greater recognition and understanding that that is perhaps the most important key towards stabilization in the Indo-Pacific. It's",
