    "turn_text": "interesting because you saw that the Japanese Navy maintain their contacts with the Philippines, even on the Duterte government. We're now seeing the Philippines now building partnerships with Indonesia, but we're definitely seeing this people. don't want to publicly choose sides, which presents challenges I want to get to in a second. But we've had these structures now. So you've got the U.S. Japan, South Korea, trilateral. And my Hudson colleague, Will Chu and I are working on a paper on the future of the Indo-Pacific, and we're sort of looking We were arguing in a sense that the U.S.-Japan South Korea trilateral could actually be a more effective engine for growth and security in the Indo-Pacific than even the Quad in some ways, given the power, the economic power, the ties that both South Korea and Japan have to South East Asia, but you've got that structure, you've got the U.S.-Japan Philippine structure, and you've got the Quad. And how do you see the relationships between these various structures in terms of complementing each other?",
