    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I mean, first and foremost, it requires the sustained energy and engagement. You know, that's key is that it can't be seen as sort of zero sum, like that, you know, America has only this much bandwidth for Indo-Pacific and it's going to start to be divided amongst these different arrangements. If things start to look that way, then you will see, you know, more tension and friction between some of these different entities. It's important for the United States to continue to show that we're growing our diplomatic presence in the Indo-Pacific, that we're investing in more, we're using different tools, whether the DFC and other components to be able to mobilize. And when it comes to these types of groups, I think it is important to show that there is for momentum new projects that are being taken and that there's not a sense of stagnation. So, you know, you're starting to see the Quad take on maritime issues, other types of things that they hadn't done before. You started to see the trilateral, build out further as well, what we just saw in Wilmington, Delaware with the Quad and other things. I think it's good to lay out that vision. But as someone who's worked at the State Department, it's hard to keep that going. especially when you have leadership change over in different nations, are they going to continue this and have the same kind of velocity? And I think that that's something that people are very much looking at towards the United States to understand whether or not we have the appetite as well, as aware with all, to be able to maintain that kind of attention. I find that to be the biggest worry amongst other nations is not our capacity, but our will. Yeah, our",
