    "turn_idx": 47,
    "turn_text": "partners in the region. But as I was laying out, I'll share this one story because it's something I keep thinking about. Last year, earlier last year, I had a leader of a Southeast Asian nation PMA call. And he asked me, like, your country's not really going to default on its debt, are you? And I remember turning to them and being like, well, look, I'm going to do my best to make sure it doesn't happen. But I can't say for certain. And the person on the other end of the phone, he kind of laughed a little bit. He just said something that's just stuck with me. He said, I just want you to know, when I gather with other officials from other nations, and we talk about America, the question we ask is, is America a reliable part? And he said that you know full well what the answer to that question is right now. And that was really striking to me. It really shows just that we can have the largest economy, the largest military in the world, but if we're not seen as reliable, we're not seen as someone who will be there for nations. And, you know, we see this question of arising immensely at the center of this question of what is the U.S. role when it comes to Ukraine, for instance. You know, there are a lot of signals being sent about American follow-through. And I find that to be the most challenging component of this, is that we see the question about deterrence, not just in terms of hard power, but in terms of that will and that determination, and frankly, the ability to make a decision. see us constantly kicking the can down the road with continuing resolutions and other issues like that. You see how divided we are, not just in Congress, but in our government. You can understand where people are coming from of like if something happens in the Taiwan Strait or elsewhere, is America going to be able to make a decision? about what to do in a timely manner. And that is something we have to, as a country, get better at to be able to show that with all of our capabilities, we also have commensurate will and ability to make those decisions.",
