    "turn_idx": 56,
    "turn_text": "into this because of September 11. That was a sophomore in college in September 11. If that day never happened, I'd probably be a mediocre microbiologist somewhere. But so having a nation come in with two wars and this war on terror, this question about transnational actors, again, challenging the Westphalian sense of nation states, it's fundamentally different than the error that we're in now. So I get it, the strategic culture has changed, but also just the paradigm shift of the world has changed. And I'm not just saying that it's some new, great power competition. That's one contour of this new era. But we don't actually know what we're in now. It wasn't necessarily just a pandemic that moved us in. This shift was already happening away from a post 9-11 era. And I think that that's something that is showing now more than ever, it's so important because we have so much unpredictability in this system. As someone who works in national security, like the thing I hate most in life is unpredictability. There's so much right now, and that's causing so much of the anxiety with the American people right now. They just don't know what's going to happen next. So therein lies the importance of having a sense of objectives. And I still feel like we struggle to understand the importance of having that. Even with the work we're doing, whether in the Indo-Pacific or in the Middle East, we talk a lot about our actions. But not a lot about what we're trying to fundamentally achieve, what that success will look like. And I think that gets harder and harder as our society gets more and more nearsighted, whether it's because of social media, whether it's because of just the overwhelming amount of information. Information is no longer the scarce resource in our society's attention. And so these are some of the threads that we're having trouble with in order to be able to lay out that kind of strategic culture.",
