  {
    "episode_id": 82263,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82263.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 62,
    "turn_text": "I was getting at when I was saying that deterrence, we need to have a broader vision about this. Because deterrence is, on some end, the other side of the coin on resilience. And this has been honestly one of the bigger areas of frustration that I've had on the select committee is because, look, I'm all for making sure we have more military might and capabilities. But we also have to look at it in terms of investing in ourselves. So my first answer to your question about how do we make sure we're doing this in a way that doesn't make us more dependent upon China? I would say things like the inflation reduction act, things like the chips and science act, investments in our own capabilities that are geared towards making us more resilient. The more resilient we are, the less dependent we are upon any nation, not just China, but any nation, to be a withstand global shocks, which affect American consumerism, prices that they have to pay, which affects public opinion, which affects political will, like the more that we can try to insulate ourselves and protect ourselves from these shocks, make our supply chains more resilient, make the production here more with us, as well as allied partners, the stronger our capacity for deterrence is. And we saw that with when it comes to sanctions on Russia, on Russian oil, and then concerns on our end about energy prices in America going up. Again, the more that we can protect ourselves from some of those shocks, the better. And I think therein lies a difference. The reason why I said I was frustrated on the slight committee side is that we have not done enough on thinking through that strategic competition and that Brazilian side. You're right. It's a select committee among, but it's not to select committee on the CCP. It's a select committee on the strategic competition between the United States and China and the Chinese Communist Party. But somehow, over the course of two years, that name has changed. not by any directive, but it's just become a select committee on the CCP. And I think we're forgetting about that sense of strategic competition. And you look at it, and look, like every single Democratic member on that select committee voted for the Chips and Science Act, whereas every single Republican member of that select committee voted against the Chips and Science Act. So you see a difference in how that perception of investment in our own capabilities, our own innovation differs between the two parties in a fundamental way. You can imagine if that's the difference on what's considered to be one of the more bipartisan parts of Congress. You can imagine how that plays out on the wider stage. So I'm trying to shift the mindset on Capitol Hill about what deterrence means. What strategic competition means is not just about like if you're in a race with another country or another person. You don't just win by slowing down them. You also can win by boosting up your own agility and capabilities. And I think more investment in America, more investment in our capabilities will put us in a better position to handle issues like climate change.",
