  {
    "episode_id": 82263,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82263.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 66,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, just in general, our industrial base in our country, we've seen just huge weakening to that, and certainly in terms of the military industrial base, too. I mean, we see that in terms of the challenge we face, in terms of being able to get artillery or equipment out to Ukraine and elsewhere. You know, when you had all these hot lines of production during the 20 years of the war on terror, and then, you know, see how we start to close that down. including, you know, the changes, fundamental changes. You know, when we look at a shift from a more Middle East focus, terrorism focused operation to something dealing with Indo-Pacific, and again, kind of near pure power competitions, it's going to require different types of assets. That being said, I'm all for the investments on the naval side, but the Ukraine war has really fundamentally shown us a change. I mean, it's one of the most incredible shifts in warfare that I've seen during my lifetime. I kind of equated to World War I, which was started by on horseback and ended with the advent of aircraft into warfare. We're seeing a level of effort in Ukraine that I think is going to fundamentally shift so much of this when you see so much of the use of drone technology, low cost, disposable technology, able to decimate exquisite military equipment, whether tanks or fighter jets or others, the change that we're going to see when it comes to hypersonic technology. You were talking about how you had a panel discussion about that. That is going to fundamentally shift naval capacity in many different ways. So we need to make sure we're doing it right. It's not just about quantity. But what are we going to do to make sure that this is going to be protected from some of these other types of efforts? Because we saw how during the war on terror, how IEDs completely reshaped our conception of warfare, that's what these drones are doing. It's the new IED mentality, and we're not responding in that way. The last thing I'll just say, because we talked about the trilateral, and this is something that I've been thinking about how we can build upon. One of the biggest hurdles for the United States to engage in greater military cooperation with Japan in particular, but also with South Korea in a societal way, is the best cybersecurity. And with all the technology that they have, Japan is is not where it should be when it comes to cybersecurity, and South Korea has more than it can do as well. And I think to the point, I'm not saying we need military interoperability, but the ability for us to partner, there is a ceiling when it comes to our capacity, especially when we are dealing with a region where you have such intel, strong, countries like China and Russia trying to penetrate every aspect of what's out there. So those are places where I continue to push for greater cooperation. I think our areas that are extremely important because it enables so much other aspects that are out there.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
