  {
    "episode_id": 82263,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82263.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 71,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, you know, what I was, what I was, what I was articulated at the time when I voted no on that was saying that the select committee is not a subcommittee of armed services. It stands separate. And as a result, it can do things that armed services and foreign affairs and all their committees cannot. I wanted to be something that could cross jurisdictions and try to bring a more fulsome approach. So when I see a list of recommendations that are almost all about military capabilities, I didn't feel like we did our due diligence. And I think that there needs to be a greater sense of what diplomatic investments as we're seeing, we are no longer, the United States is no longer the nation with the largest diplomatic presence in the Indo-Pacific. Other types of investments that we can be doing. So that's what I was pushing. And it's been a challenge. I've enjoyed my time on the select committee. I think that there have been benefits to what it can bring But I am worried about the vocabulary that it is creating on Capitol Hill that is overly simplifying what is the most complex bilateral relationship in the world that I think is going to shape so much of what happens over the next 50 years. And so I think it's important that we are as precise as humanly possible about this. And creating a situation where we're not necessarily putting forward an agenda that is actually going to make us weaker to the point we were saying earlier about coalition building. I think that that's vitally important. But coalition building, as we see with the ASEAN nations and others, may be more hesitant on certain things. And I remember hearing from them, they were saying, hey, look, if you're going to continue to push this narrative of either the US or China, they're saying, you may not like the answer to that. You got to be very careful about how it is that you're thinking about coalition building, because if you're pushing vocabulary that's very antagonistic, people might not want to join up. Might not want to necessarily be in that proximity. So I just want us to be thoughtful about that, and we can do better.",
