  {
    "episode_id": 82263,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82263.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 74,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think that that's one of the areas I feel like we've been able to make some good advances in recent years. We see a lot more conversations about what kind of security capabilities they need, but also just addressing something like listening to them about what are some of their challenges, whether that's especially on maritime, on fishing, on other types of issues. So I think that that kind of engagement is critical. It's not means doesn't mean that every single one of them is going to sign up to be, you know, write their name down on a coalition. But the more we can just do that relationship building, I think the stronger. But again, it's important that we do it. in a way that shows that we're not just thinking about them as pieces on a great power chessboard. People know when they're being used in transactional ways. And it's honestly like the way I would say they feel disrespected. if they think the only reason that you care about them is because of China. And they can tell. Now, they might try to use that to their advantage and if you're going to be in a transactional way, again, they might use that in flex in different capacities. And I hedge and try to figure out how they can maximize their leverage. So I get that. But I think there needs to be a greater purposeful understanding. And I think that's what we were talking about is like, what is the vision? You know, the title of this conversation was about, you know, free and open in the Pacific. You know, I know that's one aspect in which we can frame it. You know, the Biden administration has framed it more about security and prosperity and there are other aspects of that. But, you know, I'm not saying you necessarily have to have that perfect phrase because we know that, you know, these phrases have their own challenges and that when we understand the the, you know, that there's not a monolithic viewpoint across the Indo-Pacific. It goes different. But I think, you know, having that follow through, you know, showing that level of respect and engagement in that kind of capacity, that this is not another, this is not going to be reminiscent of, you know, island hopping from World War II, which is their understanding of the last time they got caught in the middle of great power competition. It",
