  },
  {
    "episode_id": 82263,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82263.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 83,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think that's right. Look, I think we can all agree that there are certainly actions taken by the CCP that we find to be concerning, dangerous, alarming, and I do have concerns about what happens next. So it's not meant to discount or diminish what concerns we have out there. We need to see it for what it is. But it's about trying to think through what tools that we have in our toolbox to try to address that. And yes, you're right. From the rhetoric standpoint, we want the American people to be informed. That was something that I learned from the work I did on counter-ISIS. We did not do a good enough job to explain why this was in our national interest. But I think the main thing I keep getting at is trying to understand how do we thread this needle so that we are not overly alarmist to the American people. There is a rhetoric that emerges on Capitol Hill that I think is making it seem like conflict is inevitable and imminent and I think that that's But I think that that creates a sense of concern, you know, when we saw that with a spy balloon incident, right? Like, yes, we do not want spy balloons flying over sovereign airspace of the United States, but some of the rhetoric that was coming out about that, I mean, there was a chairman of a committee that would say, no, maybe it's filled with a biological weapon. deeply dangerous to say things like that and ratchet up that kind of tension. So I just, you know, I say, look, I think that there is ways in which we can focus in on what the threat is and we should be able to articulate that in very practical ways to the American people. But lay out just the different ways in which we can counter this and address this. And I do think that this strategic competition side, right size is it. China has significant demographic challenges going forward. They are not 10 feet tall. They have huge economic problems, population demographic challenges. other issues that are going to show and manifest as we see already. But sometimes the hearings on the select committee are saying, we're losing, we're falling behind. Yes, there are places where we can do more investments, but when you look at just where our countries are, we are still in a strong and dominant place, and we shouldn't forget that. Yes, there were things we can do that shift our investments and do other things like that. I'm not saying we continue on in the status quo. But that also means that taking efforts to invest in ourselves, like the chips and science set. I try to look at our other strategic advantages. Despite our challenges as a country right now, people still want to come here, innovate here, grow businesses here. That's something that doesn't happen on mainland China. And so I've asked the select committee, is there a way that we can, in a bipartisan way, talk about how we want to make sure we can have high-skilled immigration in this country and try to detach immigration from solely a conversation about the southern border, which is unfortunately all-consuming right now. We are shooting ourselves in the foot by turning away people who want to come here and grow businesses here. And we even have people who come here and grow businesses here, and we force them to leave. And where do they go? They go back to other countries and grow competition to us. So those are the types of ways I'd like us to think about this more broadly. We can have the conversation about FMS, FMF, and military efforts to the Indo-Pacific, but we've got to be able to bring all these other aspects to bear. in a fundamental way and use what are our strategic advantages and have a sense, I don't want to say necessarily optimism per se, but a confidence in ourselves. You're not gonna be prevailing in a great power near peer competition globally if we're constantly projecting a sense of weakness and falling behind. That rhetoric actually I think hurts us when it comes to coalition building. As people would say, oh look, the select committee is talking about how we're losing this war, we're losing this race. I think there's better things we can be doing to talk about how we have a battle and a race for talent and we're winning it and we're gonna continue to engage.",
